# Excluded from the BUILD CONTEXT. This is not .gitignore — everything here is
# still tracked; it simply never reaches the builder.
#
# 🔴 `.git` IS THE ENTIRE POINT OF THIS FILE, AND IT IS NOT A SIZE OPTIMISATION.
# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so .git lands in the build context. Woodpecker
# re-clones on every pipeline and the resulting .git is BYTE-DIFFERENT each time
# (pack files, FETCH_HEAD, logs/HEAD timestamps) even for the identical commit.
# That changes the context hash, which misses the `COPY . .` layer, which misses
# every layer after it — including the expensive one.
#
# MEASURED 21-08-2026 on website-backend, rebuilding the SAME commit:
#     COPY go.mod go.sum ./   CACHED
#     COPY vendor/ vendor/    CACHED
#     COPY . .                DONE 0.3s    <- miss
#     RUN go build ...        DONE 20.1s   <- miss, because of the line above
# and isolated in a controlled A/B against the live daemon, two contexts differing
# ONLY in .git/packfile: without this file the second build MISSES, with it the
# second build is CACHED. Adding it took that repo's build step 46s -> 17s.
#
# ⚠️ This mattered not at all under kaniko, which had no layer cache configured.
# Enabling a cache is what makes .git in the context expensive.
.git
.gitignore

# Pipeline definitions never belong in an image, and excluding them means a
# CI-only change does not invalidate the image cache.
.woodpecker
.dockerignore

# ⚠️ .npmrc-ci is deliberately NOT excluded — the builder stage COPYs it.
# ⚠️ public/ is deliberately NOT excluded — it ships in the image AND deploy.yml's
#    cdn-scripts step computes SRI hashes over it (which is also why THIS repo's
#    deploy.yml does NOT get skip_clone).
